  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe broad overview notes that embedded shortwaves within NW \nflow between the Central Plains ridge and eastern seaboard \ntrough will travel SE through Tuesday morning and offer \nincreased thunderstorm chances starting this afternoon. \n\nThrough the early evening, hi-res model guidance has seemingly\nnarrowed the area of focus to northwest PA where the uncapped\nenvironment (nearing 2000 J/kg) receives enough lake-breeze \nlift to trigger initiation whereas everywhere else lacks such \nforcing mechanism to get storms started. Enough shear (around \n30kts) plus DCAPE around 800 J/kg would allow any developed \nstorm to create a downburst damaging wind threat; resultant \noutflows would then be the source of storm coverage expansion \nS/W/E through 00z. The lack of strong forcing aloft and greater \norganization methods alludes to only isolated instances of \ndamaging wind (and small hail) that would translate to a \n\"Marginal Risk\" outlook from SPC.\n\nThe \"greater threat\" leading into today was MCS potential after\n00z/8pm that has garnered the \"Slight Risk\" SPC outlook. Latest\nmodel trends more favor this threat to remain west of the region\nas it dives west into the more unstable airmass, leaving this\narea in the convective cirrus shield less conducive to severe\npotential. The approaching mid- level shortwave may still \ninitiate overnight thunderstorms but these are likely to be more\nelevated (thus greatly reducing the damaging wind risk) and \nless organized. The MCS also could produce an outflow wind that\nreaches the area that could aide convective initiation, but it\nwould still remain tough to see this reaching higher severe risk\nlevels beyond an isolated instance of damaging wind.\n\nThis is a lot to just say that afternoon storms are mainly\nfavored in NW PA with the more widespread threat being\ndetermined by the path a MCS takes after forming in the western\nGreat Lakes region.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nTiming will be key in severe weather potential Tuesday as \nforcing/lift sees a significant uptick with the passing cold \nfront and stronger shortwave trough either in the morning hours \n(lesser severe threat) or early afternoon hours (greater severe \nthreat for southeast zones). And even this simplified projection\ndoesn't account for convective evolution late tonight into early\nTuesday morning.\n\nIn the latter scenario, the general lack of overnight convection\nplus afternoon frontal arrival allows for greater buoyancy\nbuildup (approx. 1500 J/kg SBCAPE) to better manifest a\nstraight-line damaging wind threat as storm clusters form along\nthe advancing front. Severe probabilities would be lowest from\nNW PA to NE OH but slowly trend up progressing southeast toward\nthe mid-Atlantic. \n\nIn the former scenario, the combination of overnight convection\nand/or faster frontal movement results in most storm activity \noccurring during lower buoyancy hours and exits prior to Tuesday\nafternoon. An isolated damaging wind gust couldn't be ruled out\nbut is only likely to occur near Preston/Tucker Counties. The \nrest of the day may still see scattered showers and isolated \nthunderstorms due to residual lift in NW flow near the cold- \ncore upper trough center but SBCAPE values generally less than \n1000 J/kg make it difficult for severe threats to occur.\n\nEither scenario has a low-end flash flood threat thanks to\nPWATs around the 90th percentile, favoring poor drainage or\nlow-lying areas, and is more likely to occur if storms maximize\nplus repeatedly hit similar spots.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nSeasonable temperature and a trend toward dry weather is favored\nthe rest of the week as the region remains under cooler NW flow\nbetween staunch NW troughing and central plains ridging. There\nis potential for wrap-around moisture from the New England low\nto keep lower probability storm chances through Thursday\n(favoring the higher terrain) that should be monitored for\nchanges if you have planned outdoor activities.\n\nThe next widespread chance for convective activity will be\nsometime over the weekend with reinforcing upper shortwave\ntrough movement but early pattern indications suggest a pretty\nlimited hazard risk.",
